"""arr-media-guard: set the default audio and subtitle tracks of an imported mkv, and flag broken files.

  arr-media-guard                     run by Sonarr or Radarr as a CustomScript connection
  arr-media-guard --backfill <radarr|sonarr> [--apply] [--ids ID ...] [--paths PATH ...] [--limit N] [--plan-out FILE] [--workers N]
  arr-media-guard --backfill <radarr|sonarr> --sub-check [--apply] [--ids ID ...] [--paths PATH ...]
  arr-media-guard --backfill <radarr|sonarr> --apply --plan-from FILE [--canary N]
  arr-media-guard --backfill <radarr|sonarr> --plan-from FILE --only-undecided [--apply] [--plan-out FILE]
  arr-media-guard --backfill <radarr|sonarr> --convert [--apply] [--plan-from FILE] [--canary N] [--workers N] [--plex-later]
  arr-media-guard --backfill <radarr|sonarr> --convert --apply --force-convert PATH [PATH ...] [--ids ID ...]
  arr-media-guard --plex-flush <radarr|sonarr>
  arr-media-guard --backfill <radarr|sonarr> --check-audio [--ids ID ...] [--limit N] [--restart] [--workers N]
  arr-media-guard --backfill <radarr|sonarr> --check-video [--ids ID ...] [--limit N] [--restart] [--workers N]
  arr-media-guard --audit <radarr|sonarr> (--plan-from FILE | --since 24h|DATE) [--source hook|backfill] [--post]
  arr-media-guard --sub-time PATH [PATH ...] [--apply]
  arr-media-guard --subhunt radarr --ids ID [ID ...] [--apply] [--force]
  arr-media-guard --serve                   the Webhook listener for Sonarr and Radarr in Docker, see arr_serve.py
  arr-media-guard --selftest

Hook mode reads the app's radarr_* or sonarr_* variables. It answers Test at once. On a Download
event (import or upgrade) it writes one job file into the queue and returns 0 to the app. The first
event that finds no worker forks one. That worker drains the queue oldest first. With HOOK_WORKERS 1
it runs one file at a time itself. With more it runs up to HOOK_WORKERS job processes and sends every
Plex analyze itself, see coordinate(). A 200-episode pack runs at most HOOK_WORKERS processes, never 200. An empty
PLEX_URL turns every Plex call off.

For each job the worker takes the file lock, which the backfill takes too, and waits at most an
hour for it. A job process holds it shared for the checks and exclusive for the edit or a re-grab,
and re-plans when the file changed in between. A job whose file the app moved goes on at the app's
new path. A time limit covers the work up to mkvpropedit. mkvpropedit has none, so it is never
killed mid-write. The decision lives in arr_decide.py, the policy data in the JSON file
POLICY_FILE. mkvpropedit runs only when a flag must change. An undecided or dropped plan edits nothing. A file that
is not Matroska is converted into <base>.mkv with mkvmerge, proven stream by stream, and imported into the app. The
new file goes through the decision in the same job. See docs/design.md, "Conversion".
The undo record goes to the log before the edit, then the file is probed again and a final line
is logged. After an edit Plex re-analyzes the one item that holds the file. A new import may not
be in Plex yet, so the worker looks again on a 10-minute schedule, between file jobs. The analyze goes
out only after two idle checks of the item's library section 15 seconds apart. A busy section waits,
and after 30 minutes the analyze is skipped. Problems post
one Discord embed per file and problem to DISCORD_WEBHOOK: no English or original-language
audio, a short runtime, a broken duration header, a failed edit, broken audio and corrupt video.
A job whose file is gone, or that is older than a day, is logged and dropped.

When the decision abstains on the audio language, or finds none it can use, arr_lid.py hears each main audio track
whose language is not certain. Its answer enters the decision. After the edit, arr_meta.py checks the file against
TMDB and its own duration sources. The language and runtime alerts trust those checks. When the evidence adds up to
a re-grab, the worker re-grabs the download the way it does for broken audio. Its second check hears the audio and
asks TMDB again, past both caches. That needs content in REGRAB, the env file's list of re-grab kinds. Until then it
logs and posts "would re-grab" and deletes nothing. The outcome names what happened: would_regrab only when every guard passed.
status.json records the TMDB and policy checks for Zabbix.

Each text subtitle and .srt sidecar in the language of a main audio track is checked against the audio, see
docs/design.md, "Subtitle match". arr_lid.py hears two short windows, and arr_subsync.py compares the words with the
cues. A track that does not match leaves the file in a remux that the packet proof checks, or loses its flags when the
remux fails. A sidecar that does not match moves to .<NAME>-originals, or stays out of a conversion. A subtitle off by
an offset or a frame-rate ratio gets new times, and a text subtitle whose cues flash gets ends a viewer can read. The
check runs on imports, and a backfill runs it only with --sub-check. SUBTITLES in the env file sets what an import
does: off, check (it reads, reports and alerts, and changes nothing), fix (the default) or deep.

An import also times every other subtitle, other languages and PGS or VobSub pictures, against a subtitle whose words
matched, as far as its time limit allows. With SUBTITLES deep, it then queues a deep analysis of the file, which
the worker runs while no import waits: a read of the whole file for subtitle tracks the Cues do not index, one heard
window a minute, and the timing the import could not do. --sub-time runs all of it on the files it names, past the cache.

The worker also checks the audio track that will play, in three 20-second samples. A certain fault
(no audio track, silence or decode errors in all three samples, or no audio in the late sample)
skips the flag edit. When the app grabbed the file, the worker deletes it, re-monitors the item and
marks the grab failed, so the app blocklists the post and searches again. A download is one unit:
the worker samples every file it imported, deletes each broken one, re-monitors their items, then
marks the grab failed once. Clean files stay. At most 30 re-grabs per app in 24 hours (REGRAB_CAP in
the env file), after that it alerts only. Every kind of re-grab counts against it. A fault in one or two
samples alerts only.

When the audio is not certainly broken, the worker checks the video in three stages. Each runs only when the
earlier ones found nothing certain. The first compares the Matroska Segment size with the file size. The second reads
64 KiB at 256 places and looks for zero-filled pages. The third decodes three 5-second windows of the main video
stream at the audio sample positions. A certain fault skips the edit and re-grabs the download the same way, under
the same REGRAB_CAP. Its second check samples other parts of the file. A stage the job's time
limit cannot hold is skipped and logged, and an error in the check is no verdict. See docs/design.md, "Corrupt video".

An upgrade's old file waits in the app's recycle bin. When it passes the same audio and video checks, a re-grab of the
upgrade puts it back before the grab is marked failed, and the app rescans the item. With KEEP_REPLACED on, the Grab
event hard-links each file the grab may replace, and that copy stands in for a bin copy the restore cannot use. See
docs/design.md, "Restore after a bad upgrade".

--backfill --check-audio scans the whole library for broken audio, and --check-video for corrupt video, with --workers N
files at a time and a pause before each. Without --workers it takes SCAN_WORKERS from the env file, else 1. Each records
its place and resumes there on the next run, and --restart starts a new pass. Neither deletes or re-grabs. Each run posts
one summary to Discord and rewrites the list file. SIGTERM or Ctrl+C stops a scan cleanly.

--backfill walks the local app's library through its API, at nice 19 and idle I/O priority. It is
dry unless --apply is given. --ids takes movie ids for radarr and series ids for sonarr. --limit N
stops after N files that need an edit. Each file's own tracks select it, see selected(). A dry run reads --workers N
files at a time, SCAN_WORKERS by default, under the shared file lock. An apply edits one file at a time, and takes the
lock exclusive only for the edit, see backfill_file(). A backfill prints its alerts and never posts them. It asks
Plex to analyze one edited item at a time, with a pause between them, and waits while the section is busy. --plan-out writes each file's plan
as a JSON line. --plan-from limits an apply to the files that plan edits, and --canary N applies only N of
them, spread across the plan classes. --only-undecided takes the files the plan left undecided instead, so
language detection can decide them without a probe of the whole library.

--convert takes the files that are not .mkv instead and converts them into Matroska, see docs/design.md, "Conversion". With
--apply it converts CONVERT_WORKERS files at a time. --workers N wins.

--subhunt runs the subtitle hunter in arr_subhunt.py. See docs/design.md, "Subtitle hunter".

--audit reads plans and posts one summary embed with --post. With --plan-from it groups a dry run's plans
by policy path and rule, re-checks the invariants and counts the undecided and dropped plans. With --since
it reads the decision log: each edit carries the plan and invariants of the file right after the edit, and
only an edit logged without them is probed again. A nightly timer can run it over the hook's own
edits of the last 24 hours. It posts only when there is any.

Every file the hook, a backfill, the audio scan or an audit probe looks at gets one decision line in LOG,
and a one-line logfmt summary goes to syslog with the tag arr-media-guard. See docs/design.md, "Logs".
See docs/design.md, "How it runs", for the queue, the job processes and the locks.
"""
